// [MULTI-HANDLE-MANY-LEXICAL] `many` relaxes the lexical `resume` rule of
// [EFFECTS-RESUME] exactly as far as its ground extends: a lambda CONSUMED
// before the arm returns may resume, because its continuation is still live
// (that accepted shape is `src/osprey/tests/regressions/effects/staged_multiplicity`).
// A lambda that ESCAPES the arm keeps the existing rejection — here it is
// handed to a spawned fiber, so it runs after the arm has already returned and
// its continuation is dead.
//
// A `many` arm compiles and runs ([MULTI-HANDLE-MANY]), so the blanket
// [EFFECTS-RESUME] rule is now the one rejection: a `resume` written in a
// lambda body has no live continuation where the lambda runs. The arm-naming
// wording [MULTI-HANDLE-MANY-LEXICAL] specifies arrives with owned
// continuation values (plan 0016 phase 2). This golden records what the
// compiler says, so the wording cannot change silently when that lands.
effect Choice {
    control many pick: fn(int) -> int
}

fn search(seed) = perform Choice.pick(seed)

fn main() = {
    let picked = {
        handle Choice {
            pick option => {
                let escaping = |n| => resume(wrapAdd(option, n))
                let later = spawn escaping(1)
                option
            }
        }
        search(1)
    }
    print("picked=${picked}")
}
